11. Why custom Docker networks matter

Imagine your organization has:

Frontend
Backend
Database

We don't want every container communicating freely.

Instead:

                 company-network
                       |
        +--------------+--------------+
        |              |              |
        v              v              v
     frontend       backend        database

Docker's custom network provides container-to-container communication and DNS-based service discovery.

For example, one container can communicate with another using:

http://backend:8080

instead of hardcoding an IP address.

This becomes extremely important when we reach Project 2 — Docker Compose 3-Tier Application.

12. Production health check

Let's add a health check.

Stop/remove the current custom container:

docker rm -f company-web

Edit Dockerfile:

nano Dockerfile

Use:

FROM nginx:alpine

LABEL maintainer="DevOps Team"
LABEL application="Company Web Portal"

COPY website/ /usr/share/nginx/html/

EXPOSE 80

HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s --retries=3 \
  CMD wget --no-verbose --tries=1 --spider http://localhost/ || exit 1

CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]

Rebuild:

docker build -t company-web:v2 .

Run:

docker run -d \
  --name company-web \
  -p 8080:80 \
  company-web:v2

Check:

docker ps

After a short time you should see:

healthy

You can also run:

docker inspect --format='{{.State.Health.Status}}' company-web

Expected:

healthy
🔥 13. Real production troubleshooting

Now let's intentionally create failures.

Scenario 1 — Container stopped
docker stop company-web

Check:

docker ps

Troubleshoot:

docker ps -a

Start:

docker start company-web
Scenario 2 — Port already in use

Try:

docker run -d \
  --name test-web \
  -p 8080:80 \
  nginx:alpine

You should get a port conflict because 8080 is already being used.

Check:

docker ps

Find the container using port 8080.

Solution:

docker run -d \
  --name test-web \
  -p 8082:80 \
  nginx:alpine

Now:

8082 → test-web
Scenario 3 — Application not responding

Check:

docker ps

Then:

docker logs company-web

Then:

docker inspect company-web

Then:

docker exec -it company-web /bin/sh

Inside:

wget -qO- http://localhost

If Nginx is working, you'll get HTML.

Exit:

exit
